<!-- ============================================================
     SmartLoader / FakeGit MCP Supply Chain Detection Rules
     Threat: SmartLoader gang created 7,600-9,330 malicious GitHub repos
             impersonating MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers and AI skills
             (FakeGit campaign, February-April 2026). Users who install these
             trojanized packages via npm or pip trigger a postinstall hook that
             invokes a LuaJIT interpreter with an obfuscated "resource.txt"
             Lua script. The Lua stage deploys the SmartLoader downloader
             to %LOCALAPPDATA% (masquerading as AudioManager or OfficeUpdate),
             creates two scheduled tasks for persistence, queries a Polygon
             smart contract (eth_call to polygon.drpc.org) to resolve the
             current C2 server, then delivers StealC as its secondary payload.
             StealC harvests browser credentials, API keys, cloud credentials,
             SSH private keys, CI/CD tokens, and cryptocurrency wallet data.

     Source: The Hacker News (2026-02), Hexastrike threat analysis,
             SOC Prime SmartLoader analysis, Straiker blog
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK Techniques:
       T1195.001 - Compromise Software Supply Chain (TA0001)
       T1059.007 - Command and Script Interpreter: JavaScript (TA0002)
       T1036.005 - Masquerading: Match Legitimate Name or Location (TA0005)
       T1027     - Obfuscated Files or Information (TA0005)
       T1053.005 - Scheduled Task/Job: Scheduled Task (TA0003)
       T1102.002 - Web Service: Bidirectional Communication / Polygon C2 (TA0011)
       T1568     - Dynamic Resolution: blockchain dead-drop (TA0011)
       T1555.003 - Credentials from Web Browsers (StealC) (TA0006)
       T1552.001 - Credentials in Files: API keys, cloud creds (TA0006)
     Rule IDs: 104058-104065
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-20

     Campaign overview:

     The SmartLoader gang (attribution TBD, financially motivated) pivoted
     from piracy-site distribution to targeting AI practitioners and
     developers. They cloned legitimate MCP servers (notably the Oura Ring
     MCP) and created networks of fake GitHub accounts to build social proof
     for the malicious repos. Poisoned MCP registries directed developers
     to install the trojanized packages.

     Attack chain:
       1. Developer installs MCP server or AI skill from malicious GitHub repo
          via: npm install [github-url] OR pip install git+[github-urlgt 2. postinstall hook invokes: luajit.exe resource.txt
          resource.txt is a heavily obfuscated LuaJIT Lua script
       3. Lua stage deploys LuaJIT runtime to %LOCALAPPDATA%:
            %LOCALAPPDATA%\AudioManager\luajit.exe   (copy 1)
            %LOCALAPPDATA%\[OfficeUpdate-path]\luajit.exe   (copy 2)
       4. Two scheduled tasks registered masquerading as system audio or
          Office update helpers, pointing to the LOCALAPPDATA LuaJIT binaries
       5. SmartLoader calls eth_call against a Polygon smart contract via
          polygon.drpc.org; contract storage holds the current C2 IP
       6. StealC infostealer payload downloaded and executed by SmartLoader
       7. StealC sweeps: Chrome/Firefox/Edge/Brave Login Data, Cookies,
          Local State (encryption key); .env files; AWS/GCP/Azure credentials;
          npm auth tokens (.npmrc); kubeconfig; SSH private keys; crypto wallets
       8. Harvested data exfiltrated to resolved bare IP via multipart POST

     IOC hashes (SHA1 of malicious Lua scripts identified by Trend Micro):
       c36e15f0532569d789ba9fdbfccf6a1bb5ac2c75 (Trojan.LUA.SMARTLOADER)
       2a2ef9cd83bdb635bb3da2fe6b6a42c9b0cc657f (Trojan.LUA.SMARTLOADER)
       43eae0fb588987107a4805ecd1cf5c301263643b (Trojan.LUA.SMARTLOADER)

     Polygon C2 dead-drop endpoint: polygon.drpc.org
     Also observed: polygon-rpc.com, matic-mainnet.chainstacklabs.com

     Log source prerequisites (Windows):
       - Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh decoder SID 61603
         Required for rules 104058-104061
       - Wazuh FIM (syscheck) monitoring %LOCALAPPDATA% and npm/pip
         install paths; SHA1 hash computation enabled in ossec.conf
         Required for rules 104063, 104065
       - Windows EventID 4698 (Task Scheduler: task registered) or
         Sysmon EventID 1 capturing schtasks.exe
         Required for rule 104061

     Log source prerequisites (Linux/macOS):
       - auditd execve rules capturing process ancestry
         Required for rule 104064
       - Wazuh syscheck with SHA1 hashing on pip install paths and
         /tmp, /var/tmp, ~/.local
         Required for rule 104063

     Log source prerequisites (network):
       - Wazuh integration with network logs (firewall, DNS, proxy)
         decoded to fields: dst_ip, dst_host, proto, url
         Required for rule 104062

     False positive guidance:
       - LuaJIT spawned by npm: essentially zero FP rate; no legitimate
         npm package ships and invokes a Lua runtime
       - Polygon RPC connections: FP possible on dedicated blockchain
         developer workstations; scope with asset group exclusions
       - Scheduled task named AudioManager/OfficeUpdate pointing to
         LOCALAPPDATA binary: low FP; legitimate installers use
         Program Files or ProgramData paths, not AppData\Local
       - IOC hash match (rules 104063, 104065): zero FP; known-bad hashes

     Severity tiers:
       13 - LuaJIT spawned from python/pip (higher FP than npm vector)
       14 - LuaJIT spawned from npm/node; LuaJIT writing to LOCALAPPDATA;
            Polygon RPC from non-browser; schtasks.exe from LuaJIT;
            StealC-pattern credential sweep from Lua/non-browser process
       15 - Confirmed IOC hash match for SmartLoader Lua scripts
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="supply_chain,initial_access,execution,persistence,c2,credential_access,smartloader,fakegit,stealc,windows,linux,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Windows - LuaJIT spawned by node.exe / npm.cmd (T1059.007, T1195.001)
       The SmartLoader postinstall hook invokes LuaJIT with the obfuscated
       resource.txt Lua stage as its argument. No legitimate npm package
       ships a Lua interpreter or invokes an installed system lua binary.
       This is a near-certain indicator of the SmartLoader dropper stage.
       Covers: lua.exe, luajit.exe, lua52.exe, lua53.exe, lua54.exe
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104058" level="14">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:node|npm|npx)(?:\.(?:exe|cmd))?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\lua(?:jit|52|53|54)?(?:\.exe)?$</field>
    <description>SmartLoader FakeGit: LuaJIT/Lua interpreter spawned by npm/node on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user)  -  postinstall hook executing SmartLoader Lua dropper stage (T1059.007, T1195.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
      <id>T1059.007</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>smartloader,fakegit,lua_exec,npm_dropper,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Windows - LuaJIT spawned by python.exe / pip.exe (T1059.007, T1195.001)
       Same dropper vector as rule 104058 but via pip install of a
       trojanized Python package from a malicious GitHub repo. Lower
       confidence than npm vector (some Python packages legitimately
       use embedded Lua) but still unusual in enterprise environments.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104059" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:python3?|pip3?)(?:\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\lua(?:jit|52|53|54)?(?:\.exe)?$</field>
    <description>SmartLoader FakeGit: LuaJIT/Lua interpreter spawned by python/pip on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user)  -  possible pip-delivered SmartLoader postinstall hook; investigate package source (T1059.007, T1195.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
      <id>T1059.007</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>smartloader,fakegit,lua_exec,pip_dropper,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Windows - LuaJIT writing executable to LOCALAPPDATA (T1036.005, T1027)
       After the initial postinstall hook fires, the Lua stage copies the
       LuaJIT runtime to %LOCALAPPDATA%\AudioManager\luajit.exe (and a
       second masquerade path). Sysmon EventID 11 (FileCreate) captures
       this. LuaJIT writing a PE binary to AppData\Local is distinctive
       and has no known legitimate use case.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104060" level="14">
    <if_sid>61615</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\lua(?:jit|52|53|54)?(?:\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)\\AppData\\Local\\.*\.(exe|dll|lua|txt)$</field>
    <description>SmartLoader FakeGit: LuaJIT writing file to LOCALAPPDATA on $(win.eventdata.computer)  -  SmartLoader deploying payload or runtime copy to masquerade path (T1036.005, T1027)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1036.005</id>
      <id>T1027</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>smartloader,fakegit,localappdata_write,masquerade,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Windows - schtasks.exe spawned by LuaJIT (T1053.005)
       SmartLoader registers two scheduled tasks for persistence after
       deploying to LOCALAPPDATA. schtasks.exe being invoked as a child
       of luajit.exe is a unique and highly specific indicator - no
       legitimate Lua application creates Windows scheduled tasks.
       Covers both "schtasks.exe /create" and "schtasks /run" invocations.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104061" level="14">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\lua(?:jit|52|53|54)?(?:\.exe)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\schtasks\.exe$</field>
    <description>SmartLoader FakeGit: schtasks.exe spawned by LuaJIT on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user)  -  SmartLoader registering scheduled task for persistence (AudioManager/OfficeUpdate masquerade) (T1053.005)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.005</id>
      <id>T1036.005</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>smartloader,fakegit,scheduled_task,persistence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Network - LuaJIT or non-browser process connecting to Polygon RPC
       (T1102.002, T1568)
       SmartLoader resolves its active C2 IP by calling eth_call against
       a Polygon smart contract via polygon.drpc.org or related Polygon
       JSON-RPC endpoints. Non-browser processes have no legitimate reason
       to query Polygon blockchain nodes. Requires network log integration
       with fields dst_host or url decoded from proxy/firewall logs.

       Note: if_group is not used here; rule matches on network log data
       decoded to dst_host or dns.question.name fields.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104062" level="14">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="dst_host|dns.question.name|network.destination.domain" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:polygon\.drpc\.org|polygon-rpc\.com|matic-mainnet\.chainstacklabs\.com|polygon-mainnet\.infura\.io|polygon-bor\.publicnode\.com|polygon\.llamarpc\.com)</field>
    <field name="process.name|src_process|application" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:chrome|firefox|msedge|brave|safari|chromium|opera|vivaldi|iexplore)</field>
    <description>SmartLoader FakeGit: non-browser process connecting to Polygon JSON-RPC endpoint on $(agent.name)  -  SmartLoader resolving C2 via Polygon smart contract eth_call dead-drop; blockchain-based C2 is unsinkholable via DNS (T1102.002, T1568)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
      <id>T1568</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>smartloader,fakegit,polygon_c2,blockchain_c2,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       FIM - Known SmartLoader Lua script IOC hashes (T1195.001)
       Wazuh syscheck detects the three known SmartLoader Lua script
       hashes (classified as Trojan.LUA.SMARTLOADER.THBBDBE by Trend
       Micro). These are SHA1 hashes of resource.txt variants found
       across the 109+ malicious GitHub repos. Fires on any path.
       Requires syscheck SHA1 hash computation enabled in ossec.conf:
         [hash_algorithm]sha1[/hash_algorithm]
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104063" level="15">
    <if_sid>550,553,554,593</if_sid>
    <regex>c36e15f0532569d789ba9fdbfccf6a1bb5ac2c75|2a2ef9cd83bdb635bb3da2fe6b6a42c9b0cc657f|43eae0fb588987107a4805ecd1cf5c301263643b</regex>
    <description>SmartLoader FakeGit: IOC hash match for SmartLoader Lua dropper script (Trojan.LUA.SMARTLOADER) detected by FIM on $(agent.name)  -  confirmed SmartLoader supply chain compromise; full IR response warranted (T1195.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
      <id>T1059.007</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>smartloader,fakegit,ioc_hash,lua_dropper,critical_ioc,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Linux/macOS - LuaJIT spawned by npm/node/pip/python via auditd
       (T1059.007, T1195.001)
       Same LuaJIT postinstall hook vector as Windows rules 104058-104059
       but captured via Linux auditd execve syscalls. The lua/luajit binary
       being executed with npm, node, or pip as the parent process is
       equally anomalous on Linux/macOS as on Windows.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104064" level="14">
    <decoded_as>auditd</decoded_as>
    <field name="audit.syscall">execve</field>
    <field name="audit.exe" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:^|/)(?:npm|node|npx|python3?|pip3?)$</field>
    <field name="audit.cmd" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:^|/)lua(?:jit|5\.[1-4])?(?:\s|$)</field>
    <description>SmartLoader FakeGit: LuaJIT/Lua spawned by npm/node/pip on $(agent.name)  -  postinstall hook invoking Lua interpreter; likely SmartLoader supply chain compromise (T1059.007, T1195.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1195.001</id>
      <id>T1059.007</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>smartloader,fakegit,lua_exec,linux,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       FIM - LuaJIT binary written to LOCALAPPDATA AudioManager path
       (T1036.005) - Windows
       Detects Wazuh syscheck alerts where a file named luajit.exe or
       lua*.exe is created in a path containing AudioManager, AudioService,
       OfficeUpdate, or MicrosoftOffice (SmartLoader's known masquerade dirs).
       Requires FIM monitoring of %LOCALAPPDATA% (%USERPROFILE%\AppData\Local).
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104065" level="15">
    <if_sid>550,553,554,593</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">(?i)\\AppData\\Local\\(?:AudioManager|AudioService|SoundCore|MicrosoftOffice|OfficeUpdate)\\lua(?:jit|52|53|54)?(?:\.exe)?$</field>
    <description>SmartLoader FakeGit: LuaJIT binary created in LOCALAPPDATA masquerade directory (AudioManager/OfficeUpdate) on $(agent.name)  -  confirmed SmartLoader payload deployment to persistence path (T1036.005)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1036.005</id>
      <id>T1053.005</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>smartloader,fakegit,localappdata_lua,masquerade,critical_ioc,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
